  "common_questions": [
    {"q": "What does Omnemo do?", "a": "It gives every AI coding agent on an Omarchy computer one shared memory. Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Crush and Gemini all talk to the same store over MCP, so what one saves, the others can recall."},
    {"q": "How do I install it?", "a": "Follow INSTALL.md: a Python virtual environment, `pip install git+https://github.com/GuyMannDude/omnemo`, a link into ~/.local/bin, then `omnemo setup`. No sudo. Or ask your Omarchy agent: \"Install Omnemo — follow INSTALL.md.\""},
    {"q": "Can I pip install omnemo?", "a": "No. The PyPI package is 0.1.1 and out of date. Install from GitHub main as INSTALL.md shows."},
    {"q": "Where are my memories stored?", "a": "In ~/.local/share/omnemo/store.db, on your computer. Omnemo never sends them anywhere. To wipe everything, copy that folder somewhere safe and delete it."},
    {"q": "Which agents does it work with?", "a": "Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Crush and Gemini CLI get the MCP server. Pi gets the memory skill only; its MCP registration is not supported yet."},
    {"q": "I installed it but my agent does not see the tools.", "a": "Start a NEW session: one that was already open when setup ran does not see the server. Then run `omnemo setup` again and read its rows; any FAIL row says what to fix. For Gemini, the server shows as Disabled until you trust the folder you run Gemini in."},
    {"q": "Codex says it cannot install it.", "a": "Codex's default sandbox has no network and cannot write the config folders. Start it for this one job with `codex -s danger-full-access -a on-request`, or pick full access with /permissions, then switch back after."},
    {"q": "How do I make the agent forget something?", "a": "Ask it to (\"forget that I…\"). It finds the memory and calls forget with its id. From a terminal: `omnemo search WORD` to find the id, then `omnemo forget ID`. Forget deletes it for good."},
    {"q": "Something I told it changed. Will it keep giving the old answer?", "a": "Saving the new fact returns the older look-alikes as near_duplicates; saving again with `supersedes` set to their ids means recall only gives the new one. From the shell: `omnemo save \"new fact\" --supersedes 12`."},
    {"q": "Can I change how it ranks or how fast memories fade?", "a": "Yes, in ~/.config/omnemo/config.toml: recall weights, the 30-day base half-life, limits, near-duplicate settings, and per-category importance and half-life. You can add your own categories."},
    {"q": "How much memory and disk does it use?", "a": "Measured on a 2016 two-core laptop: about 330 MB of RAM per running server (each open agent runs its own), about 2.1 MB of disk at 1,000 memories, warm recall around 140–170 ms."},
    {"q": "How do I uninstall it?", "a": "`omnemo bar off`, then `omnemo disconnect --all-pieces`, then remove ~/.local/bin/omnemo and ~/.local/share/omnemo-venv. Run disconnect first: Codex refuses to start while its required Omnemo entry points at a deleted program. Your memories in ~/.local/share/omnemo stay until you delete them."}
  ],

  "why": "Every AI helper on your computer forgets you when you close it, and none of them knows what the others learned. Omnemo gives them one shared memory on your own computer: tell one of them something worth keeping, and next time, in a new chat or even a different AI app, it already knows.",

  "install_steps": [
    {"step": 1, "instruction": "If your AI helper is Codex, start it with full access for this one job (`codex -s danger-full-access -a on-request`). If it is OpenCode, sign in with `opencode auth login` yourself first."},
    {"step": 2, "instruction": "Open your AI helper (`omarchy agent`) and say: \"Install Omnemo — follow INSTALL.md.\" It runs the steps and checks each one."},
    {"step": 3, "instruction": "When it says it is done, close the helper and open a new session. Ask it: \"Use the omnemo MCP server to recall: when was omnemo installed?\" It should answer with today's date."}
  ],

